WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:05.820
Three hundred metres below the
North Sea, in cold, total darkness,

2
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:08.903
a steel structure is
holding back a reservoir.

3
00:00:09.043 --> 00:00:12.518
Inside, the pressure is
measured in hundreds of bar.

4
00:00:12.757 --> 00:00:16.317
There is nobody down there to
turn a wheel, or pull a lever.

5
00:00:16.657 --> 00:00:21.087
Yet this machine opens and closes,
measures, injects chemicals,

6
00:00:21.177 --> 00:00:25.166
and, if something goes wrong,
shuts the well in, all by itself.

7
00:00:25.707 --> 00:00:28.051
This is the subsea Christmas tree.

8
00:00:28.691 --> 00:00:30.651
Let's find out how it works.

9
00:00:31.791 --> 00:00:35.479
By the end of this video, you
will be able to do five things.

10
00:00:35.519 --> 00:00:40.039
Find the tree in a subsea production
system, and name its four jobs.

11
00:00:40.179 --> 00:00:43.995
Identify the main valves, and
explain what each one does.

12
00:00:44.135 --> 00:00:48.740
Explain why the valves are fail-safe
closed, and how they are controlled.

13
00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.779
Describe how the tree
works as a well barrier.

14
00:00:51.919 --> 00:00:55.884
And recognise the trees used
on the Norwegian shelf today.

15
00:00:57.024 --> 00:00:59.197
Let's start with the big picture.

16
00:00:59.287 --> 00:01:03.477
Most subsea wells on the Norwegian
shelf are tied back to a host:

17
00:01:03.707 --> 00:01:08.197
a platform, a floating production
vessel, or, like Snøhvit,

18
00:01:08.237 --> 00:01:12.212
a plant onshore, 143 kilometres away.

19
00:01:12.353 --> 00:01:15.403
On the seabed, wells are
grouped in templates.

20
00:01:15.603 --> 00:01:19.625
Each well has a wellhead, and on
top of every wellhead sits a tree.

21
00:01:19.765 --> 00:01:23.625
Oil and gas flow up from the
reservoir, through the tubing,

22
00:01:23.665 --> 00:01:27.677
through the tree, and into the
flowline that carries them to the host.

23
00:01:27.817 --> 00:01:32.027
Alongside runs an umbilical,
bringing electrical power,

24
00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:37.009
communication, hydraulic fluid and
chemicals down from the host to the tree.

25
00:01:37.249 --> 00:01:42.388
So the tree is the gateway between the well
below, and the production system above.

26
00:01:43.427 --> 00:01:45.686
A subsea tree has four jobs.

27
00:01:45.826 --> 00:01:47.536
One: control the flow.

28
00:01:47.586 --> 00:01:50.005
How much comes out of
the well, or goes in.

29
00:01:50.245 --> 00:01:52.045
Two: isolate the well.

30
00:01:52.175 --> 00:01:55.170
The tree is a pressure-containing
safety barrier.

31
00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:59.397
Three: inject chemicals, and
measure pressure and temperature.

32
00:01:59.637 --> 00:02:03.794
And four: give access to the well,
for maintenance and intervention.

33
00:02:04.234 --> 00:02:09.245
And the name? The stack of valves and
branches simply looks like a Christmas tree.

34
00:02:10.285 --> 00:02:11.946
Let's open one up.

35
00:02:12.036 --> 00:02:16.986
This is a vertical tree, the design
Equinor now uses as its standard,

36
00:02:17.086 --> 00:02:21.143
for example at Johan Castberg
and on Troll Phase 3.

37
00:02:21.283 --> 00:02:25.718
At the bottom, a hydraulic connector
locks the tree onto the wellhead.

38
00:02:25.857 --> 00:02:28.647
Inside the wellhead
sits the tubing hanger.

39
00:02:28.937 --> 00:02:33.641
It carries the weight of the production
tubing, and seals off the space around it.

40
00:02:33.881 --> 00:02:36.609
Two separate channels
run up through the tree.

41
00:02:36.699 --> 00:02:40.899
The production bore carries the well
fluids, straight up from the tubing.

42
00:02:41.039 --> 00:02:46.370
The annulus bore connects to the annulus:
the space between the tubing and the casing.

43
00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:50.580
Each bore has its own valves:
a master valve at the bottom,

44
00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:54.877
a wing valve leading out to the
side, and a swab valve at the top.

45
00:02:55.117 --> 00:02:59.018
The production wing leads through
a choke, and out to the flowline.

46
00:02:59.959 --> 00:03:03.039
Nearly every valve on the
tree works in the same way,

47
00:03:03.079 --> 00:03:05.162
so let's look at one in detail.

48
00:03:05.301 --> 00:03:09.348
It is a gate valve: a flat
steel gate with a hole in it,

49
00:03:09.388 --> 00:03:11.293
sliding between two metal seats.

50
00:03:11.433 --> 00:03:14.588
When the hole lines up
with the bore, fluid flows.

51
00:03:14.728 --> 00:03:18.651
Slide the gate across, and the
solid steel blocks the flow.

52
00:03:18.891 --> 00:03:21.991
The gate is moved by a hydraulic actuator.

53
00:03:22.241 --> 00:03:26.271
Fluid pushes on a piston, and
the piston pushes the gate open,

54
00:03:26.311 --> 00:03:28.382
compressing a powerful spring.

55
00:03:28.621 --> 00:03:30.471
Here is the key idea.

56
00:03:30.571 --> 00:03:33.201
Hydraulic pressure holds the valve open.

57
00:03:33.371 --> 00:03:38.048
Take the pressure away, for any reason,
and the spring drives the gate shut.

58
00:03:38.288 --> 00:03:40.483
We call this fail-safe closed.

59
00:03:40.723 --> 00:03:42.533
Lose the signal? Closed.

60
00:03:42.573 --> 00:03:44.153
Lose the hydraulic supply?

61
00:03:44.193 --> 00:03:45.811
Closed. Lose everything?

62
00:03:45.851 --> 00:03:46.851
Still closed.

63
00:03:47.061 --> 00:03:49.341
The default state of the well is safe.

64
00:03:50.381 --> 00:03:55.050
Now let's meet the valves, one at a
time, starting with the production bore.

65
00:03:55.691 --> 00:03:58.151
First, the production master valve.

66
00:03:58.311 --> 00:04:01.832
It is the main door between the
well and the rest of the system.

67
00:04:01.972 --> 00:04:05.142
During production it stays open.

68
00:04:05.182 --> 00:04:09.649
We avoid operating it while the well
is flowing, to protect its seals,

69
00:04:09.689 --> 00:04:13.788
and many trees have two master
valves in series, for extra security.

70
00:04:14.428 --> 00:04:16.518
Next, the production wing valve.

71
00:04:16.558 --> 00:04:18.351
This is the working valve.

72
00:04:18.491 --> 00:04:22.431
Whenever we need to shut the well
in, the wing valve does the job,

73
00:04:22.471 --> 00:04:25.635
so the master valve is
kept in perfect condition.

74
00:04:26.275 --> 00:04:28.775
Then the choke. It is
not a shut-off valve.

75
00:04:28.815 --> 00:04:30.176
It is the throttle.

76
00:04:30.316 --> 00:04:34.816
By adjusting the opening, we set the
flow rate, and the pressure upstream.

77
00:04:35.126 --> 00:04:39.896
The flow is fast and abrasive, so
choke parts use tungsten carbide,

78
00:04:39.936 --> 00:04:43.007
and the insert can be replaced by an ROV.

79
00:04:43.647 --> 00:04:46.317
At the top of the bore is the swab valve.

80
00:04:46.527 --> 00:04:50.417
It gives straight vertical access
to the well for wireline tools,

81
00:04:50.457 --> 00:04:52.796
and stays closed during production.

82
00:04:53.436 --> 00:04:57.508
The annulus bore has its own
master, wing and swab valves.

83
00:04:57.598 --> 00:05:02.158
The master valve is normally open,
so we can monitor annulus pressure.

84
00:05:02.428 --> 00:05:07.131
When the well heats up, the trapped
fluid expands, and that pressure rises.

85
00:05:07.771 --> 00:05:11.886
Linking the two sides is the
crossover valve, normally closed.

86
00:05:11.976 --> 00:05:15.664
Open it, and annulus pressure
can be bled into the flowline.

87
00:05:16.304 --> 00:05:18.714
Then the chemical injection valves.

88
00:05:18.854 --> 00:05:22.211
They let the umbilical feed
chemicals into the well stream.

89
00:05:22.351 --> 00:05:27.681
At Snøhvit, glycol, or MEG,
is injected at the wellhead,

90
00:05:27.781 --> 00:05:32.695
so hydrates cannot block the
143 kilometre pipeline to shore.

91
00:05:33.335 --> 00:05:37.895
One more valve belongs to the family,
even though it sits deep in the well:

92
00:05:37.995 --> 00:05:39.988
the downhole safety valve.

93
00:05:40.128 --> 00:05:44.608
A hydraulic control line runs from
the tree, through the tubing hanger,

94
00:05:44.648 --> 00:05:45.648
down to the valve.

95
00:05:45.748 --> 00:05:49.648
Same principle: hydraulic
pressure holds a flapper open,

96
00:05:49.688 --> 00:05:52.007
and a spring closes it.

97
00:05:52.047 --> 00:05:55.132
Lose the pressure, and the flapper
snaps shut, sealing the tubing.

98
00:05:56.072 --> 00:05:58.416
So how do the valves know what to do?

99
00:05:58.556 --> 00:06:00.826
The tree has no brain of its own.

100
00:06:01.056 --> 00:06:05.646
Commands come from the master control
station, on the host or onshore,

101
00:06:05.686 --> 00:06:07.513
and travel down the umbilical.

102
00:06:07.653 --> 00:06:11.533
At the tree, a subsea control
module receives each command.

103
00:06:11.823 --> 00:06:17.765
Its electronics energise a small solenoid
valve, which lets hydraulic fluid flow to

104
00:06:17.805 --> 00:06:19.682
one specific actuator.

105
00:06:19.823 --> 00:06:23.362
Pressure builds, the spring
compresses, and the valve opens.

106
00:06:23.501 --> 00:06:28.362
Sensors report back: pressure,
temperature, and the state of every valve.

107
00:06:28.553 --> 00:06:31.903
And remember, it is pressure
that keeps the valves open.

108
00:06:32.123 --> 00:06:36.166
Cut the power, or vent the
hydraulics, and the springs take over.

109
00:06:36.356 --> 00:06:41.246
If the control system is ever lost,
an ROV can connect to the tree,

110
00:06:41.286 --> 00:06:43.329
and operate the valves directly.

111
00:06:44.369 --> 00:06:46.379
Why so much redundancy?

112
00:06:46.549 --> 00:06:50.404
Because of one of the most important
principles in well engineering.

113
00:06:50.544 --> 00:06:55.517
There must always be two independent, tested
barriers between the reservoir and the

114
00:06:55.557 --> 00:06:56.621
environment.

115
00:06:56.861 --> 00:06:59.961
Here is a simplified producing well.

116
00:07:00.001 --> 00:07:03.356
The primary barrier, in blue,
is the tubing and packer,

117
00:07:03.396 --> 00:07:05.456
closed by the downhole safety valve.

118
00:07:05.596 --> 00:07:09.026
The secondary barrier, in
red, is the casing and cement,

119
00:07:09.066 --> 00:07:12.697
the wellhead, the tubing hanger,
and the tree, with its valves.

120
00:07:12.937 --> 00:07:17.265
If one barrier fails, the other
still holds, and we have time to act.

121
00:07:17.455 --> 00:07:20.895
That is why barrier elements,
including the tree valves,

122
00:07:20.935 --> 00:07:24.215
are tested regularly, to
prove they still work.

123
00:07:25.255 --> 00:07:28.965
Let's put it all together, and
follow one well through start-up,

124
00:07:29.005 --> 00:07:30.970
and an emergency shutdown.

125
00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:36.669
Start-up. From the control room,
operators open the valves in sequence:

126
00:07:36.709 --> 00:07:39.981
the downhole safety valve
first, then the master valve,

127
00:07:40.021 --> 00:07:41.021
then the wing valve.

128
00:07:41.082 --> 00:07:45.367
Finally the choke opens gradually,
and the well begins to flow.

129
00:07:45.907 --> 00:07:47.577
Now something goes wrong.

130
00:07:47.697 --> 00:07:51.344
A gas leak on the host
triggers an emergency shutdown.

131
00:07:51.485 --> 00:07:52.685
Pause for a moment.

132
00:07:52.725 --> 00:07:54.618
Which valve should close first?

133
00:07:57.458 --> 00:08:01.167
The wing valve. It is designed
to close against the flow.

134
00:08:01.407 --> 00:08:03.317
Next, the master valve.

135
00:08:03.447 --> 00:08:06.907
And only when the flow has
stopped, the downhole safety valve,

136
00:08:06.947 --> 00:08:08.658
the last line of defence.

137
00:08:08.898 --> 00:08:11.128
No operator action is needed.

138
00:08:11.268 --> 00:08:14.570
The hydraulics are vented,
and the springs do the rest.

139
00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:18.061
Trees come in two basic designs.

140
00:08:18.201 --> 00:08:21.861
In a vertical tree, the tubing
hanger sits in the wellhead,

141
00:08:21.931 --> 00:08:24.193
and the tree is installed on top.

142
00:08:24.383 --> 00:08:27.783
In a horizontal tree,
the tree goes on first.

143
00:08:27.993 --> 00:08:32.487
The tubing hanger lands inside it,
and the production flows out sideways.

144
00:08:32.727 --> 00:08:39.067
The big advantage: the completion can be pulled
without lifting the tree off the wellhead.

145
00:08:39.107 --> 00:08:45.140
Norne, in 1997, was the first field on the
Norwegian shelf to use horizontal trees.

146
00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:50.540
Today, Equinor's standard is a vertical
tree, developed with Aker Solutions.

147
00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:54.800
It comes in two variants,
seven-by-five and seven-by-seven,

148
00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:59.690
with the same interfaces, and
a flow module sets its duty:

149
00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:02.389
producer, gas injector or water injector.

150
00:09:02.529 --> 00:09:07.249
Aker Solutions says the design is
roughly half the weight of earlier trees,

151
00:09:07.359 --> 00:09:10.689
which makes it easier to
install from smaller vessels,

152
00:09:10.759 --> 00:09:12.852
an advantage in the Barents Sea.

153
00:09:13.892 --> 00:09:17.260
Subsea trees are everywhere
on the Norwegian shelf.

154
00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:22.120
At Troll, in the North Sea, subsea
templates feed Troll B and Troll C,

155
00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:26.315
and since 2021, Phase 3
has fed gas to Troll A.

156
00:09:26.455 --> 00:09:32.124
At Åsgard, in the Norwegian Sea, the world's
first subsea gas compression plant started

157
00:09:32.164 --> 00:09:33.642
up in 2015.

158
00:09:33.781 --> 00:09:38.618
At Aasta Hansteen, trees stand
in 1,300 metres of water,

159
00:09:38.658 --> 00:09:40.008
the deepest on the shelf.

160
00:09:40.148 --> 00:09:45.618
And in the Barents Sea, Johan Castberg
began producing in March 2025,

161
00:09:45.738 --> 00:09:49.425
with thirty subsea wells,
all on the new standard tree.

162
00:09:50.465 --> 00:09:51.915
Time to test yourself.

163
00:09:51.955 --> 00:09:53.278
Three questions.

164
00:09:53.519 --> 00:09:57.655
Question one. What does fail-safe
closed mean for a tree valve?

165
00:10:03.295 --> 00:10:08.050
B. The valve closes automatically
when hydraulic pressure is lost.

166
00:10:08.489 --> 00:10:13.201
Question two. In an emergency
shutdown, which valve closes last?

167
00:10:18.841 --> 00:10:22.657
C. The downhole safety valve,
the last line of defence.

168
00:10:23.097 --> 00:10:28.157
Question three. Which valve do you open,
together with the annulus master valve,

169
00:10:28.197 --> 00:10:30.860
to bleed annulus pressure
into the flowline?

170
00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:38.609
B. The crossover valve.

171
00:10:39.849 --> 00:10:41.254
Let's recap.

172
00:10:41.295 --> 00:10:45.624
The tree controls the flow, isolates
the well, injects and monitors,

173
00:10:45.664 --> 00:10:46.903
and gives access.

174
00:10:47.043 --> 00:10:51.633
Its valves are held open by hydraulic
pressure, and closed by springs,

175
00:10:51.673 --> 00:10:53.398
so the well fails safe.

176
00:10:53.537 --> 00:10:57.597
In an emergency: wing valve,
master valve, and last of all,

177
00:10:57.637 --> 00:10:59.486
the downhole safety valve.

178
00:10:59.627 --> 00:11:04.957
And together with the casing and the
wellhead, the tree forms the second of the two

179
00:11:04.997 --> 00:11:07.390
barriers that keep the
reservoir where it belongs.

180
00:11:07.729 --> 00:11:09.518
Thank you for watching.

